  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n- Hot, dry, and smoky conditions continue through this week.\n\n- Even hotter, drier, and windy conditions to end next week.\n\n- Potential cool down to near average temperatures next weekend,\n  though it remains dry. \n\nOur current pattern of high pressure ridge to our east and the\nNorthern Rockies under southwest flow will remain unchanged for\nthe time being. Expect very warm and dry days, unstable \natmosphere, and smokey conditions. Our region looks to be cut off \nfrom monsoonal flow but isolated thunderstorms during the \nafternoons in southwest MT and Lemhi County ID will be possible \nbut low probability.\n\nThe biggest concern in the short term will be degrading air\nquality from local and regional fires. Each overnight smoke will\nlikely filter into valleys, then during the afternoon solar\nheating will destabilize the atmosphere allowing for some\nventilation. Unfortunately, smoke inherently stabilizes the \natmosphere by blocking solar energy and limiting surface heating. \nWithout a cold front to scour out valleys, eventually smoke will \nincrease to the point of stabilizing valleys, degrading visibility\nand air quality. High resolution guidance would suggest northwest\nand west-central MT will be among the first valleys for this to \noccur over the next few days. \n\nRelief? The models are notorious for trying to breakdown high\npressure ridges too quickly. Current models would suggest \nsuccessive short waves and eventually ejection of the main \nPacific low pressure trough flattening the ridge, could happen as\nearly as Friday/ Saturday. Last night I wrote Thursday/ Friday, \nso the models are already trying to delay ridge breakdown. \n\nWhen breakdown of the ridge does happen, this would likely lead to\na period of heightened fire weather due to an increase in winds.\nAdditionally, a cooling of temperatures and cleaning out of smoke\nfrom valleys would also be logical outcomes.",
